 "about": "An early C18 brick house of 3 storeys and 5 bays on the High Street, now offices; the front faces map east (x 11). OSM's block 355779095 was cut into units by the address step: Westbrooke is u2 and u3 joined, 14 m of front for its 5 bays.",
 "evidence": [
  "List entry 1094199 (OGL): 'House, now offices. Circa early C18. Altered and enlarged at the right and rear. Main front of 3 storeys and 5 bays wide, the central one advanced somewhat. Brick; slate roof. Ground floor with central door in pedimented stone doorcase approached up modern steps and flanked on either side by 2 sash windows. 1st and 2nd floors each with 5 similar windows, the centre one on the 2nd floor with a cut brick head. Deep modillioned eaves cornice.'",
  "No photo to look at: Commons has none of the house by name, and Historic England's are view-only (not used). Everything here is from the list text, the LiDAR shell and the map.",
  "Extent: OSM draws one block (355779095, 34 x 29 m) for the whole row; the address step cut it into units. The listing's own polygon is only a marker. 5 bays need about 14 m: u2 (7 m, where the marker falls) and u3 south of it. 'Enlarged at the right' is north (the right hand, seen from the High Street): u1, left as it is. A guess from the list text: check on site or a free photo if one turns up.",
  "Front: bays at y 1, 4, 7, 10, 13; the door at y 7, where the map's paving meets the front (y 7-13). The advanced centre bay (y 6-8) is drawn by its brick returns (break_start, break_end), not a step in the wall; the cut brick head over the centre window of the 2nd floor; the modillioned cornice along the whole front under the eaves. The pedimented doorcase is Build's (design.py: doorcase), at the front door.",
  "Inside: an entrance hall (x 6-10, y 6-8) to the staircase behind (x 0-5, y 5-9), entered on every storey beneath a depressed arch with panelled soffit and jambs (stair_arch, on the hall's west edge); the stair's balustrade of alternate fluted and twisted balusters along the flight's open side; the seven Doric frieze sections on the hall and stair walls (6 on the ground floor, 1 on the landing); painted fielded panelling in the front rooms of the ground and first floors.",
  "Not built: the modern steps (they would stand on the pavement); the carved stair ends; the staircase ceiling's plasterwork (the game shows no ceilings); windows on the ends, and on the back where the unit behind (355779095u6, y 3-11) stands against it - the back's corners have theirs; the listing's 'contemporary doors' (Build's stand in). The roof: slate, pitched along the front - the listing gives no form and the LiDAR read it as flat."
 ],
